# 50 MHz on the CORE clock (period 20.000000 ns) -- hardware clock, and the
# frequency this build is allowed to be reported at.
#
# Hardware chain: 27 MHz on D18 -> GTP_PPLL I=1/M=1/F=50/O0=27 -> 27 x 50/27 = 50.000 MHz.
# VCO = 27 x 50 = 1350 MHz (legal 1330..2133), PFD 27 MHz.
#
# Why there are TWO clocks below
# ------------------------------
# The placer stops as soon as it meets the declared period, so declaring exactly
# the target frequency is the worst way to ask for that frequency.  `core_clk` is
# the real hardware clock and is the row the frequency claim is read from;
# `core_clk_pnr` is the same physical clock declared one rung tighter (52 MHz)
# with `-add`, purely so the placer does not settle early.  Both are analysed and
# the compliance evidence is the `core_clk` row: 50 MHz, 0 failing setup
# endpoints at 20.000 ns.  A core_clk_pnr violation is not a compliance failure.
create_clock -name board_clk -period 37.037037 -waveform {0 18.5185185} [get_ports {clk}]
create_clock -name core_clk -period 20.000000 -waveform {0 10.000000} [get_pins {u_core_pll/CLKOUT0}]
create_clock -name core_clk_pnr -period 19.230769 -waveform {0 9.6153845} -add [get_pins {u_core_pll/CLKOUT0}]
